{-
   The native half of a run and of the slice over it - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5.

   Growing a run and handing its storage back, which is the part that needs an allocator, and the
   `Flat(a)` descriptor, which is two fields here and three on JS. `Run.yana` beside this is what a
   run and a slice *are*; `Run.js.yana` is the other descriptor.

   **A native file, and that is what it is saying** - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5. `resize` asks
   `allocateHeap` for slots and `releaseRun` hands them back to `freeHeap`, and both of those are
   `Heap.native.yana`'s: a target with no addresses has no heap of this kind and no run to grow. On
   JS a container is the host's own array, which grows itself.

   The `Reclaim` instance is here rather than beside the type for the same reason and not for a
   weaker one: what it does *is* release the allocation, so an instance on a target with no
   allocation would be an instance with nothing to say. `Run(a)` still exists on JS - it is the
   descriptor a compiler-placed run is - and it simply has no `Reclaim`, which is the correct answer
   rather than an empty one.
-}

{-
   The bytes `capacity` slots need, from the allocator - and null where they cannot be had.

   **The half a compiler can place, alone in a function of its own.** What a run needs is storage of
   a size, and the only thing about it that the allocator has to decide is *where*: everything else
   about a run - what the descriptor holds, when it grows, who hands it back - is the two functions
   below and is the same wherever the bytes came from. Splitting the request out that way is what
   lets a stage with something better than the general heap to offer replace this one call and leave
   the rest of the run alone. `resolve_ssa/opt/place.cpp` is the stage that does, and
   `known.def`'s `runSlots` row is how it finds this.

   `probe` is never read. It is how the element type reaches a function whose arguments would
   otherwise not name one - `byteSpan` measures against it - and it is why a run's slots can be
   asked for without the caller computing a byte count of its own.

   It answers `%a` and not the allocator's `%U8` for the same reason it takes the probe: what was
   asked for is slots of a type, so what comes back is a pointer to one. The cast is a line of this
   body rather than a line of every caller, which is the difference between a boundary crossed once
   and one crossed at every literal - and a cast at the caller is a *call* until the lowering, which
   is one more thing between a placement and the pointer it is about.

   `@noinline` on purpose, and `newRun` below is the opposite on purpose: the guards and the
   allocator are what a *refusal* costs, and a refusal is the rare arm, so inlining them at every
   literal would put three blocks and a call in front of code that wants three field writes. It is
   a directive rather than a preference for a second reason - a compiler that replaces this call
   has to still be able to *find* it, and a body spliced into its caller is not a call any more.
-}
@builtin(runSlots)
@noinline
pub fn runSlots(probe: %a, capacity: Size) -> %a:
    if capacity == 0 then return null()
    if capacity > maxCount then return null()

    return bitcast(allocateHeap(byteSpan(probe, capacity))) :: %a

{-
   The descriptor over slots somebody else placed - the other half, and the one that is compiler
   magic in no stage at all.

   `owns` is the placement's answer and arrives as an argument for exactly that reason: whoever
   decided where the bytes are is the only thing that knows whether this run is anyone's to hand
   back, and a constant written here would be a second opinion. `newRun` passes `runFromHeap`
   because it asked the allocator; a stage that put the slots in the frame passes `runBorrowed`, and
   `Reclaim` below then folds to nothing with no rule of its own about literals.

   A null pointer is the refusal, and it answers a run with **no room** - whose capacity says
   exactly what it has, so the caller that needed the room tests for it. See `push`.

   The refusal is a capacity of zero rather than an arm of its own, and that is worth saying because
   this body is inlined at every run in the program. Written as `if isNull then emptyRun()` it is a
   branch, a second construction and a join at each of them; written this way it is one choice
   between two numbers, which the optimizer answers outright wherever the placement is known. What
   makes the shorter form correct is that a null pointer with no capacity is unreachable rather than
   unsafe - nothing may index past zero - and that `freeHeap` answers a null pointer by returning,
   so the flag beside it says the truth either way.
-}
@inline(always)
pub fn runOver(capacity: Size, slots: %a, owns: HeapFlag) -> Run(a):
    let room = if isNull(slots) then 0 :: Size else capacity
    return Run {items: slots, capacity: truncate(room) :: Count, ownsHeap: owns}

{-
   A run of `capacity` slots, placed by the compiler - and the body a stage that cannot place one
   reaches instead.

   **A role and a body on one declaration**, which is not a contradiction: the hook is what a call
   site expands to and the body is what a call that cannot be expanded reaches. The shipping stage
   always expands - an allocation with a count, placed by the escape analysis that places every
   other one - so this body is dead there. The ownership resolver expands nothing here and reaches
   this body at every run it builds; what places the run for it is `runSlots` above being one call
   in an inlined body rather than a heap buried three functions deep.

   It is here rather than in `Run.yana` because a heap is what it asks for: `allocateHeap` is
   `Heap.native.yana`'s, and a target with no addresses has neither it nor a run to grow.
   `Run.js.yana` carries the same role with no body, which is the arrangement `blockCopy` uses.

   `@inline(always)` and two calls, which is the whole body: what a caller gets from this is the
   *allocation* in its own frame, where a pass can see the count it asked for and where the pointer
   goes. Sized at what it reads as - the guards, the allocator and the refusal arm - it was over
   every budget there is, and a literal's buffer was a call whose storage nothing above could name.
-}
@builtin(newRun)
@inline(always)
pub fn newRun(capacity: Size) -> Run(a) =
    runOver(capacity, runSlots(null() :: %a, capacity), runFromHeap)

{-
   Room for `wanted` slots, relocating if there is not.

   `&self` is what makes relocation safe with no new rule: a mutable borrow of the run conflicts with
   any live borrow into it, so the checker already rejects holding an element borrow across one of
   these. False means the allocator refused and the run is exactly as it was.

   The whole of the old capacity is copied rather than the live prefix, because a run does not know
   which of its slots are live - that is what §2 means by having no notion of occupancy. It costs a
   constant factor on a growth that is amortized anyway, and it is what keeps the primitive from
   needing a count it would then have to be told about.

   A `wanted` past what `Count` can hold is refused rather than truncated, which is the same answer
   an allocator that said no gets and for a better reason: storing it would leave a run whose
   capacity field disagreed with the storage behind it, and every later `resize` would read the
   masked number back and believe it.

   `@cold`, and it is a statement about what this *is* rather than about one of its callers: a
   relocating growth is the amortized arm of every container that has one - `growArray` and
   `indexRebuild` are both of them here - and a run that already has the room returns on the first
   line. What the attribute buys is the copy *in*: `allocateHeap` and `moveMemory` are each worth
   expanding into a body nothing weighs as rare, and expanding them here puts a bump-pointer path
   and a four-register block move into code that runs once per doubling.
-}
@cold
pub fn resize(&self: Run(a), wanted: Size) -> Bool:
    let room = self.capacity :: Size
    if wanted <= room then return True

    -- Refused one line before the allocator is asked, and for a different reason than a refusal from
    -- it: there is nowhere to write the new base. A `runFixed` run's slots are its owner's own bytes
    -- and the two words this body reads are a descriptor built for the call
    -- (Implementation-Containers.md §7.2's tier-1 borrow), so relocating would leave the elements the
    -- caller can still reach exactly where they were and this descriptor pointing at a block nothing
    -- frees. That is what makes `@capacity(n)` a bound rather than a hint. The comparison folds
    -- wherever the flag is a constant, which is everywhere the run was not built in a generic body.
    if self.ownsHeap == runFixed then return False

    if wanted > maxCount then return False

    let fresh = bitcast(allocateHeap(byteSpan(self.items, wanted))) :: %a
    if isNull(fresh) then return False

    if room > 0:
        copyMemory(bitcast(fresh) :: %U8, bitcast(self.items) :: %U8, byteSpan(self.items, room))

    if self.ownsHeap == runFromHeap then freeHeap(bitcast(self.items) :: %U8)

    self.items = fresh
    self.capacity = truncate(wanted) :: Count
    self.ownsHeap = runFromHeap

    return True

{-
   The placement test - Implementation-Storage.md §5.

   Written as a plain function taking the run by borrow as well as as the instance below, because a
   container built on runs has to be able to release its own without owning it out of itself: moving
   a field out of the value an authored `Reclaim` was handed is a partial move, and duplicating this
   comparison in every such container is the thing worth avoiding. It is storage release and nothing
   else, which is what makes it a permitted call inside an authored `Reclaim` - see
   checkReclaimShape.

   Inline, frame and region storage release nothing: the owner's own bytes, the frame returning, and
   the region closing are what hand those back. Only the allocator's is this function's to give
   back, which is why the three are one value here. When the bit is a constant the escape analysis
   patched - which it is everywhere the run was not built inside a generic body - the comparison
   folds and this whole function with it.

   A `Reclaim` and not a `Drop`, which is what keeps a container built on runs region-placeable: a
   region discharges every `Reclaim` inside it in bulk, and handing storage back is exactly the kind
   of thing that may happen in bulk at a point the author did not choose.

   **This is one comparison and not a call**, which is Implementation-Containers.md §13.2's first
   step: a callee taking an aggregate by the default convention is a memory-typed value parameter,
   and the inliner re-roots those at the caller's own place (`Binding::Memory` in opt_inline.cpp),
   so this test is spliced into whatever reads it and the teardown holding it is spliced into the
   `drop` that runs it.

   And it *folds*, which is what §2 claims: the tag is a constant in the literal's own `Run`
   allocation and reaches the array through whole-aggregate copies, so opt_place.cpp carries what is
   known across them rather than rewriting either side. A
   frame-placed array's teardown is no instructions at all. What still tests at run time is an array
   something wrote through a borrow, and a grown one - where the question is real.
-}
@builtin(releaseRun)
pub fn releaseRun(self: Run(a)):
    if self.ownsHeap == runFromHeap then freeHeap(bitcast(self.items) :: %U8)

instance Reclaim(Run(a)):
    fn reclaim(->value: Run(a)): releaseRun(value)

{-
   `values` and `valuesMut` **were here and are now `Core/Array.native.yana`'s.**

   They read `ArrayView`'s `items`, and that record is `@local` in `Core`: a field of a `@local`
   record may be named in the module that declares it and nowhere else, so the one place a pointer
   into a window is given the window's provenance has to be beside the record rather than beside the
   heap. The argument for them being in `Native` was that what they hand out is a raw pointer and
   handing one out is this module's business - which is true of `stringBytes` and of every other
   declaration here, and is not enough on its own to put a reader of another module's private
   representation on this side of the import.
-}
